<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Gibbon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Gibbon was a British essayist, historian and politician, born at Putney, Surrey, in 1737. Removed from Magdalen College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, after his conversion to Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1753, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and later wrote that the idea of his history first came to him among the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in October 1764. The History of the Decline and Fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> appeared in six volumes between 1776 and 1789, known for the quality and irony of its prose, its use of primary sources and its polemical criticism of organised religion. He sat in the House of Commons for Liskeard and died in 1794 at Fletching, East Sussex, while staying with Lord Sheffield.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon.jpg" alt="Edward Gibbon" width="330" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Walton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Gibbon_by_Henry_Walton_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gibbon was the son of Edward and Judith Gibbon, born at Lime Grove in Putney. His five brothers and one sister all died in infancy, and his mother died while he was a boy. He was raised partly by his aunt Catherine Porten, whom he later credited with giving him a taste for books. By 1751 his reading already included Laurence Echard's Roman History and the Universal History from the Earliest Account of Time. After 14 months at Magdalen College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he converted to Roman Catholicism on 8 June 1753 and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, where his father's threat of disinheritance led him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> on Christmas Day 1754.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first book, Essai sur l'Étude de la Littérature, appeared in 1761. He served as an officer in the South Hampshire Militia, commissioned on 12 June 1759, and later said that the drill of a modern battalion gave him a clearer notion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and the legion. In 1764 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and he later recalled that the idea of a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> came to him in the city, although scholars doubt the exactness of his account. After his father's death in 1770 he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Dr Johnson</a>'s Literary Club.</p><p>In 1774 he entered the House of Commons for Liskeard, where he was a silent supporter of the Whig ministry; he lost the seat in 1780 and was returned for Lymington the next year. The first volume of the Decline and Fall was published on 17 February 1776 and went through three editions in 1777. Volumes II and III followed on 1 March 1781, and he finished the work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> on 27 June 1787. The last three volumes reached the press in May 1788, timed to his 51st birthday, and he was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> that November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last years were marked by gout and a swelling of the scrotum, probably a hydrocele; the last of three operations in early January 1794 brought on peritonitis, and he died at 12:45 pm on 16 January 1794 aged 56. He was buried in the Sheffield Mausoleum at Fletching.</p><p>Chapters XV and XVI, on the Christian church, led to attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-priestley">Joseph Priestley</a>, Richard Watson and Henry Edwards Davis, and to his Vindication of 1779. Scholars now say that he attributed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s fall chiefly to luxury and the loss of martial character, not to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> alone. He insisted on drawing from primary sources, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> modelled much of his own prose style on him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Gibbon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians since <b>Edward Gibbon</b> have called the Vandal conquest of North Africa the greatest single blow to the Western Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Adam Smith, David Hume, <b>Edward Gibbon</b>, Voltaire and Jean-Jacques Rousseau are counted the founding fathers of anti-mercantilist thought.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean Bodin, Francis Bacon, Spinoza, Rousseau, Hume, <b>Edward Gibbon</b> and Adam Smith are among those who noted the importance of his realism, and he is thought to have influenced Montaigne, Descartes, Hobbes, Locke and Montesquieu.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;His reign is marked by the rare advantage of furnishing very few materials for history; which is, indeed, little more than the register of the crimes, follies, and misfortunes of mankind.&#8221;</p><footer>The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire (1776), chapter 3 &#183; original &#183; of the reign of Antoninus Pius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_the_Decline_and_Fall_of_the_Roman_Empire_(Bury)/III" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The vast extent of the Roman empire was governed by absolute power, under the guidance of virtue and wisdom.&#8221;</p><footer>The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire (1776), chapter 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_the_Decline_and_Fall_of_the_Roman_Empire_(Bury)/III" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The various modes of worship which prevailed in the Roman world were all considered by the people as equally true; by the philosopher as equally false; and by the magistrate as equally useful.&#8221;</p><footer>The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire (1776), chapter 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_the_Decline_and_Fall_of_the_Roman_Empire_(Bury)/II" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
